{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-sonnet-4-6","id":"msg_01Wo4MzhrFh6AuXDVjrf4NMq","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"The current implementation uses a 2D DP table `int[n+1][sum+1]`, which is O(n * sum) space. I need to reduce it to O(sum) by using a 1D DP array.\n\nThe current 2D DP logic:\n- `dp[i][j] += dp[i-1][j]` — carry forward from previous row (without using coin i)\n- `dp[i][j] += dp[i][j - coins[i-1]]` — use coin i (unbounded, from same row)\n\nFor a 1D array, we can iterate over coins in the outer loop and sums in the inner loop:\n- `dp[j] += dp[j - coin]` for each coin\n\nThis is the standard 1D coin change approach where we iterate through coins in the outer loop and amounts left to right in the inner loop, which naturally handles the unbounded case and ensures we count combinations rather than permutations.\n\nLet me trace through an example with coins {1,2,3} and sum 5 to verify this works correctly. Starting with dp[0] = 1, I process each coin sequentially. When I add coin 1, every amount becomes reachable in exactly one way. Then coin 2 opens up new combinations—for instance, dp[5] goes from 1 to 3 as I can now make 5 using combinations like {2,2,1} and {2,3}. Finally, coin 3 adds even more combinations, bringing dp[5] to 5 total ways: {1,1,1,1,1}, {1,1,1,2}, {1,2,2}, {1,1,3}, {2,3}.\n\nNow I'm updating the CoinChangeDPService class to implement this algorithm.","signature":""}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":3,"cache_creation_input_tokens":13847,"cache_read_input_tokens":15934,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":13847},"output_tokens":8,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"diagnostics":{"cache_miss_reason":{"type":"system_changed","cache_missed_input_tokens":12283}},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"0197bb11-4152-4f64-8d94-93f2f58c16de","uuid":"3c9